
  "In the Hindu Namakarana ceremony, the name is chosen based on the sound the newborn made at birth. The name does not define the child — it only addresses them. Does this change your understanding of what `x = 10` means? Is `x` defining 10, or is `x` a way of addressing 10?",
  "When you write `a = b = []` in Python, both names refer to the same list. In the naming ceremony, when multiple people call a child by the same nickname, they all see the same child. How does shared reference create dependency? If one person sees the child laugh, do all who share the name see it too? How does this mirror the bug where `a.append()` affects `b` when `a is b`?",
  "Python small integers (-5 to 256) are 'interned' — the same object is reused for all occurrences of the same value. So `x = 100; y = 100; x is y` is True. But for larger integers or other objects, `x is y` is False even if `x == y` is True. Philosophically: does shared identity matter more than shared value?",
  "What happens to the integer object `10` after you run `x = 10; x = 20`? Does `10` cease to exist? If Python's garbage collector is running, it may reclaim `10`. But if `10` is still in use somewhere, it stays. Is this like a child's memory of their birth name after they grow up — it may linger in memory even after the identity changes?",
